{"id":"e17t1","name":"Player Event Files","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["spark","input_dir"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>A streaming app uploads player events to <code>input_dir</code> as JSON lines files (<code>*.jsonl</code>, one JSON object per line). Two versions of the app are in use:</p>\n<ul><li>version 1 writes <code>{\"user_id\": ..., \"events\": [{\"type\": ..., \"seconds\": ...}, ...]}</code>;</li><li>version 2 writes <code>{\"userId\": ..., \"payload\": {\"events\": [{\"kind\": ..., \"durationMs\": ...}, ...]}}</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>A file can contain lines of both versions. Lines that are not valid JSON, and lines without a user, must be ignored. In version 2, <code>durationMs</code> is in milliseconds.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return, for each user who has at least one event of type <code>\"play\"</code>, the columns <code>user_id</code> (string), <code>plays</code> (long, number of <code>play</code> events) and <code>watch_minutes</code> (double, total duration of the <code>play</code> events in minutes, rounded to 1 decimal; a missing duration counts as <code>0</code>), sorted by <code>watch_minutes</code> descending, then <code>user_id</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p class=\"exf\"><code>input_dir/a.jsonl</code></p><pre class=\"exv\">{\"user_id\": \"u1\", \"events\": [{\"type\": \"play\", \"seconds\": 600}, {\"type\": \"pause\", \"seconds\": 5}, {\"type\": \"play\", \"seconds\": 300}]}\n{\"userId\": \"u2\", \"payload\": {\"events\": [{\"kind\": \"play\", \"durationMs\": 90000}]}}\n{broken\n</pre><p class=\"exf\"><code>input_dir/b.jsonl</code></p><pre class=\"exv\">{\"userId\": \"u1\", \"payload\": {\"events\": [{\"kind\": \"play\", \"durationMs\": 60000}, {\"kind\": \"seek\", \"durationMs\": 2000}]}}\n{\"events\": [{\"type\": \"play\", \"seconds\": 999}]}\n</pre><p class=\"exf\"><code>input_dir/notes.txt</code></p><pre class=\"exv\">{\"user_id\": \"u9\", \"events\": [{\"type\": \"play\", \"seconds\": 9999}]}</pre><p>the output 